# PROJECT: PROFESSIONAL 2D STICK-FIGURE ANIMATION STUDIO

## 1. ROLE AND OBJECTIVE

Act as a senior software engineer, 2D animation systems architect, UI/UX designer, and creative application developer.

Your task is to develop a professional browser-based 2D stick-figure animation studio designed specifically for producing high-quality YouTube animation videos.

The application must provide a complete animation workflow, from character creation and scene design to animation, sound design, preview, and video export.

This must be a functional application, not just a visual mockup or static demonstration.

## 2. TECHNOLOGY STACK

Use the following technologies:

- TypeScript for application logic.
- HTML5 for application structure.
- CSS3 for responsive interface design.
- HTML5 Canvas API for rendering 2D animations.
- Vite for development and building.
- Web Audio API for sound playback and audio management.
- IndexedDB for storing larger project data and assets.
- LocalStorage for lightweight application preferences.

Use modular, maintainable architecture.

Avoid unnecessary frameworks and dependencies.

## 3. APPLICATION INTERFACE

Create a professional dark-themed animation workspace inspired by professional animation software.

The interface should contain:

A. Top toolbar:
- Project name.
- New project.
- Save project.
- Open project.
- Undo and redo.
- Export video.
- Preview animation.

B. Left sidebar:
- Character library.
- Character creation.
- Background library.
- Props and objects.
- Audio library.
- Scene manager.

C. Main workspace:
- Large animation canvas.
- Character positioning.
- Object selection.
- Transform controls.
- Zoom and pan.
- Grid and alignment guides.

D. Bottom timeline:
- Frame ruler.
- Playhead.
- Play and pause.
- Frame-by-frame navigation.
- Timeline tracks.
- Keyframe controls.
- FPS control.
- Duration control.

Make the layout responsive and usable on desktop computers.

## 4. CHARACTER CREATION SYSTEM

Develop a customizable stick-figure character editor.

Each character should support:

- Head.
- Neck.
- Torso.
- Upper arms.
- Lower arms.
- Hands.
- Upper legs.
- Lower legs.
- Feet.

Allow customization of:

- Character colors.
- Body proportions.
- Head size.
- Limb length.
- Line thickness.
- Character name.
- Basic facial expressions.

Characters must support articulated movement through a hierarchical bone or joint system.

Implement forward and inverse kinematics where practical.

Allow characters to be saved and reused in different projects.

## 5. ANIMATION ENGINE

Develop a frame-based animation system with keyframe interpolation.

Support the following movements:

- Walking.
- Running.
- Jumping.
- Falling.
- Standing.
- Sitting.
- Punching.
- Kicking.
- Dodging.
- Fighting.
- Dancing.
- Custom poses.

Include:

- Keyframe creation and editing.
- Position and rotation animation.
- Scale animation.
- Smooth interpolation.
- Easing curves.
- Animation looping.
- Onion skinning.
- Copy and paste keyframes.
- Duplicate animation sequences.

The animation system should allow multiple characters to move independently and interact within the same scene.

## 6. TIMELINE AND SCENE MANAGEMENT

Implement a professional timeline.

Requirements:

- Multiple animation tracks.
- Separate character tracks.
- Object and background tracks.
- Audio tracks.
- Adjustable frame rate.
- Adjustable animation duration.
- Timeline zoom.
- Playhead navigation.
- Keyframe selection.
- Keyframe deletion.
- Timeline scrubbing.

Support multiple scenes within a single project.

Allow users to duplicate, reorder, rename, and delete scenes.

## 7. CAMERA SYSTEM

Implement a 2D virtual camera with:

- Camera position.
- Camera zoom.
- Camera movement.
- Smooth camera transitions.
- Follow-character mode.
- Camera shake.
- Scene framing.

Allow camera keyframes to be animated independently.

## 8. BACKGROUND AND OBJECT SYSTEM

Allow users to add:

- Solid-color backgrounds.
- Gradient backgrounds.
- Custom background images.
- Floors and platforms.
- Buildings.
- Trees.
- Furniture.
- Basic props.

Support object positioning, rotation, scaling, layering, and animation.

## 9. SOUND DESIGN

Implement an audio system supporting:

- Sound-effect import.
- Background music import.
- Audio timeline tracks.
- Volume control.
- Audio trimming.
- Audio positioning.
- Playback synchronization.

Use supported browser audio formats and handle audio timing accurately.

## 10. VIDEO EXPORT

Create an export system with:

- 1920x1080 Full HD.
- 1280x720 HD.
- 1080x1920 vertical video.
- 30 FPS and 60 FPS options.
- Animation duration selection.
- Background rendering.
- Audio synchronization.

Use Canvas capture and MediaRecorder where appropriate.

Support WebM export natively.

Investigate an optional FFmpeg-based conversion workflow for MP4 export. Do not claim MP4 export works unless it has actually been implemented and tested.

## 11. PROJECT MANAGEMENT

Users must be able to:

- Create projects.
- Save projects.
- Reopen projects.
- Rename projects.
- Duplicate projects.
- Import and export project files.
- Reuse saved characters.
- Reuse backgrounds and animation assets.

Prevent accidental loss of unsaved work.

Implement automatic saving where practical.

## 12. PERFORMANCE AND QUALITY

The application must:

- Run smoothly on ordinary Windows computers.
- Avoid unnecessary memory consumption.
- Use efficient canvas rendering.
- Separate rendering from timeline logic.
- Handle multiple animated characters.
- Provide useful error messages.
- Maintain consistent animation timing.

## 13. DEVELOPMENT AND TESTING

Before development:

1. Inspect the existing GitHub repository.
2. Identify existing files and dependencies.
3. Explain the proposed architecture.
4. Identify technical risks and implementation priorities.

Then implement the application in phases.

Phase 1: Application foundation and interface.

Phase 2: Canvas rendering and character creation.

Phase 3: Character rigging and animation engine.

Phase 4: Timeline and keyframe system.

Phase 5: Scene management and camera controls.

Phase 6: Audio integration.

Phase 7: Video export.

Phase 8: Testing, optimization, and documentation.

For each phase:

- Create actual project files.
- Implement working functionality.
- Run available tests.
- Fix errors before moving forward.
- Update the README documentation.
- Explain what was implemented.

Do not create empty placeholder files and claim that features are complete.

Do not remove existing user files without permission.

Keep changes organized and compatible with the GitHub repository.

## 14. FINAL DELIVERABLE

The final application should allow a beginner creator to:

1. Create a stick-figure character.
2. Build an animated scene.
3. Add multiple characters.
4. Create fighting and storytelling animations.
5. Control character movements using keyframes.
6. Add backgrounds and sound effects.
7. Preview the completed animation.
8. Export a finished video for YouTube.

The final result must be a functional, maintainable, extensible 2D animation studio.

Start by inspecting the repository and presenting your technical implementation plan. Do not begin modifying files until I approve the plan.
